"""A dated public forecast register; no outcome is inferred from missing evidence."""
from pathlib import Path
from html import escape
import json

ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
BASE = '/issues/2026-q4/'

def render_forecasts():
    record = json.loads((ROOT/'forecasts/data/forecast-register-2026-10-30.json').read_text())
    forecasts = record['forecasts']
    assert all(f['status'] == 'draft' and f['outcome'] is None and f['locked_at'] is None for f in forecasts), 'Update the renderer for a new forecast cohort before publishing outcomes.'
    cards = []
    for f in forecasts:
        short = f['id'].split('-')[-1]
        probability = round(100*f['probability'])
        refs = ', '.join(f'<a href="{BASE}sources/#source-{n}">[{n}]</a>' for n in f['refs'])
        cards.append(f'''<article class="forecast-card" data-horizon="{escape(f['horizon'])}" id="{short.lower()}">
<div class="forecast-meta"><span>{short} / {escape(f['horizon'])}</span><span class="forecast-status">Workshop draft</span></div>
<h3>{escape(f['title'])}</h3><p class="forecast-claim">{escape(f['claim'])}</p>
<div class="forecast-probability"><span><strong>{probability}%</strong> editorial probability</span><span>Subjective judgment</span></div>
<div class="forecast-bar" aria-hidden="true"><span style="width:{probability}%"></span></div>
<dl class="forecast-dates"><div><dt>Outcome window</dt><dd>{f['start']} to {f['end']}</dd></div><div><dt>Evidence cutoff</dt><dd>{f['evidence_cutoff']}</dd></div></dl>
<p><strong>Why this direction:</strong> {escape(f['rationale'])}</p><p><strong>What could overturn it:</strong> {escape(f['risk'])}</p>
<details><summary>Baseline, sources &amp; exact test</summary><p><strong>Starting point:</strong> {escape(f['baseline'])} {refs}</p><p><strong>The test:</strong> {escape(f['rule'])}</p><p class="forecast-record-id">{f['id']} · Version {f['version']} · Created {f['created_on']} · Not locked</p></details></article>''')
    pilots = ''.join(f'<tr><th scope="row">{escape(f["label"])}</th><td>{escape(f["claim"])}<br><strong>Against:</strong> {escape(f["against"])}</td><td>Open<br>No probability recorded</td></tr>' for f in record['pilots'])
    return f'''<div class="reading-toolbar"><a href="{BASE}">← Issue 01</a><span>Permanent department / October 30, 2026</span></div>
<section class="forecast-hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">The next season</p><h1>Expectations.<br><em>Open to evidence.</em></h1><p class="forecast-deck">Dated predictions about AI’s next quarter and next year. A public record of what we expect, what happens, and what changes our minds.</p><p class="forecast-draft-note"><strong>Workshop outlook.</strong> Six candidates for Q1 and calendar 2027. The probabilities are editorial judgments, not measured statistics or a tested model. Final issue forecasts have yet to be locked.</p><div class="issue-actions"><a class="button" href="#outlook">Explore the forecasts ↓</a><a href="#scorecard">View the scorecard</a></div></div><figure><img src="/assets/issue-01/sealed-forecasts.webp" width="1500" height="500" alt="An imagined natural-history illustration of sealed forecast envelopes, awaiting a later review."><figcaption>Keep the original envelope. Let the evidence open it.</figcaption></figure></section>
<aside class="concept-intro"><p class="concept-label">AI concept</p><p>A forecast says what outcome is expected and when. Its probability expresses uncertainty. Its test states what evidence will decide it. Keeping these together allows a later result to challenge the original prediction fairly.</p></aside>
<section id="outlook" class="forecast-outlook"><div class="contents-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Six draft calls / two horizons</p><h2>Where might the habitat change?</h2></div><a href="{BASE}the-next-season/">Read the full outlook →</a></div><p>Q1 2027 means January–March. The annual horizon covers January–December 2027. Later evidence cutoffs allow time for results to be published; they do not extend the event window. All drafts were created October 30, 2026.</p>
<div class="forecast-filter" hidden><label for="forecast-horizon">Show forecasts for</label><select id="forecast-horizon"><option value="">Both horizons</option><option>Next quarter</option><option>Next year</option></select><span id="forecast-filter-status" role="status">6 draft forecasts</span></div>
<div class="forecast-grid">{''.join(cards)}</div><p class="forecast-note">The two business-adoption forecasts share a topic and may move together. These are six calls, not six independent experiments. Company growth does not establish AI causation; provider reports do not establish independently verified capability.</p></section>
<section id="scorecard" class="forecast-scorecard"><p class="eyebrow">The naturalist’s scorecard</p><h2>Reopen the envelopes.</h2><p>When outcomes are due, every original prediction will sit beside the evidence and the verdict. Misses remain part of the record.</p><div class="forecast-metrics"><div><strong>0</strong><span>Locked issue forecasts</span></div><div><strong>0</strong><span>Resolved locked forecasts</span></div><div><strong>Not yet available</strong><span>Hit rate &amp; probability error</span></div></div>
<div class="forecast-empty"><p><strong>No completed track record yet.</strong> Six workshop candidates and three open October pilots are not evidence of accuracy. There is no prior resolved issue cohort for a quarter-to-quarter comparison.</p><p>Future panels will show hits and misses with their denominator, average probability error, pending and unresolvable cases, and what changed our understanding. Quarterly and annual results will remain separate.</p></div>
<details><summary>How will accuracy be measured?</summary><p><strong>Hit rate</strong> is correct directional calls divided by resolved directional calls. A forecast above 50% favors yes; below 50% favors no. Exactly 50% has no directional call.</p><p><strong>Binary Brier error</strong> measures the gap between a probability and an outcome: (probability − outcome)², with outcome 1 for yes and 0 for no. Average it across resolved eligible forecasts. It ranges from 0 (best) to 1 (worst). A 70% forecast scores 0.09 if the event happens and 0.49 if it does not. This is an illustration, not an AIEVOQ result.</p><p>Missing evidence is not a miss. Pending and unresolvable records remain visible but unscored. The original locked probability is retained, and each forecast counts once. A confidence chart will wait until enough outcomes accumulate; a few hits cannot establish well-judged confidence.</p><p><a href="{BASE}the-naturalist-s-scorecard/">Read the scoring rules and academic sources →</a></p></details>
<h3>The October pilot envelopes</h3><p>Recorded October 27, 2026. Public evidence cutoff: December 31, 2026. These earlier editorial forecasts have no numerical probabilities and will be reviewed separately from the finalized issue cohort.</p><div class="forecast-table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Original October pilot forecasts; scroll horizontally on small screens"><table><thead><tr><th>Envelope</th><th>Original wording and test</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody>{pilots}</tbody></table></div><p><a href="{BASE}the-three-sealed-envelopes/">Read their complete original context →</a></p></section>
<section class="forecast-archive"><p class="eyebrow">The inspectable record</p><h2>Dates stay attached.</h2><p>Before the 2027 window begins, a new dated edition will lock the final claims, probabilities and tests. This October draft will remain available. Later revisions and withdrawals must stay visible; forecasts will never be backdated.</p><ul><li><a href="/forecasts/data/forecast-register-2026-10-30.json">October 30 register · JSON</a></li><li><a href="/forecasts/data/forecast-register-2026-10-30.csv">Six draft forecasts · CSV</a></li><li><a href="/forecasts/data/methodology-2026-10-30.md">Resolution and scoring rules · text</a></li></ul><p>Related reading: <a href="{BASE}the-next-season/">The next season</a> · <a href="{BASE}the-naturalist-s-scorecard/">The naturalist’s scorecard</a> · <a href="/statistics/">The statistical census</a></p></section>'''
